Reach the fallback for DEC private modes libvterm does not model

set_dec_mode() drops an unrecognised mode with a debug log and returns, so
vterm_screen_set_unrecognised_fallbacks never sees it. As with the
private-leader CSIs in 0001, that makes such modes *unreachable* rather
than merely unhandled: an application cannot implement one itself no
matter what it registers.

DEC 2026 (synchronised output) is the live example -- a terminal cannot
support it on top of libvterm's parser, because the sequence never
arrives.

The consult is at the TOP of the function rather than in the default
branch, and that placement is the whole of it: it is what lets an
application refuse a mode libvterm DOES model, not merely implement one
it does not. A terminal built on libvterm cannot otherwise decline
bracketed paste, the mouse modes or focus reporting -- libvterm records
the mode as set and then answers DECRQM saying so, which advertises a
feature the layer above is not honouring.

It was in the default branch first, which handled DEC 2026 and nothing
else. Moving it up subsumes that case and keeps its behaviour exactly:
an application that declines gets libvterm's own handling, including
the debug log for a mode nobody claimed.

Applied on top of 0001 at build time (see the top-level Makefile);
upstream is otherwise untouched. Worth sending upstream.

--- a/src/state.c
+++ b/src/state.c
@@ -775,6 +775,21 @@ static void set_modes(VTermState *state, int num, int val)
 
 static void set_dec_mode(VTermState *state, int num, int val)
 {
+  /* Offer every DEC private mode to the application-level fallback
+   * before acting on it.
+   *
+   * Two things depend on this and neither is reachable otherwise. A mode
+   * libvterm does not model -- DEC 2026, synchronised output -- cannot be
+   * implemented above it at all, because the sequence never arrives. And
+   * a mode libvterm DOES model cannot be declined: it would be recorded
+   * as set here and then reported as set by DECRQM, which tells a client
+   * a feature is on while the layer above ignores it. */
+  if(state->fallbacks && state->fallbacks->csi) {
+    long fallback_args[1] = { num };
+    if((*state->fallbacks->csi)("?", fallback_args, 1, NULL, val ? 'h' : 'l', state->fbdata))
+      return;
+  }
+
   switch(num) {
   case 1:
     state->mode.cursor = val;
